course: religion-ethics
lesson: 51
title: Life begins: being born and growing up
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every person who ever lived started as something smaller than a full stop, grew for about nine months inside their mother's womb, and was then born helpless, unable even to hold up their own head.
explain: Picture Sunday lunch at Ellie's house: her aunt's new baby wraps his whole hand round Ellie's finger; nine years ago Ellie was that small too, and so was every grown-up at the table.
explain: Babies are born able to suck, cry and grip, but walking, talking and reading are learned slowly, by trying, falling and trying again; a giraffe walks within an hour, while a human baby needs about a year, because our big brains have so much to learn.
explain: Christians at Ellie's church believe every baby is known and loved by God and is a gift, not a thing anyone owns; other faiths welcome babies with their own blessings, non-religious families may hold a naming day, and everyone agrees a new life is a wonder.
explain: A new brother or sister does not use up a family's love: love is not a cake cut into smaller slices, it grows to cover everyone, even when the baby takes up a lot of time.
fact: A baby grows inside the mother's body, in the womb, for about nine months before it is born.
fact: A baby starts when a tiny cell from the mother and a tiny cell from the father join together; before that, the baby did not exist as a person.
fact: Some children are adopted: they join a family that did not give birth to them, and they are just as much part of it.
fact: Newborn babies can suck, cry and grip, but walking and talking are learned slowly; many babies take their first steps at around 11 to 13 months.
fact: Different animals grow at different speeds: a giraffe calf stands and walks within about an hour, but a human baby needs about a year.
fact: Kittens are born with their eyes shut; their eyes open after about one or two weeks.
fact: A seed is not dead: it holds a tiny resting plant and a store of food, and water and warmth wake it up.
fact: A frog changes shape as it grows: frogspawn, tadpole, froglet, frog; it is the same animal all the way through.
fact: Babies born early often need a special warm hospital cot to help them breathe and feed; most grow up strong, though sometimes it is harder.
fact: A birthday marks the day a person was born; some groups, such as Jehovah's Witnesses, choose not to celebrate birthdays, and that choice is respected.
fact: Christians call every child a gift from God and baptise babies or thank God for them; Jesus told of the tiny mustard seed that grows into a big plant.
fact: Parents' love does not run out when a new baby comes, even though a newborn needs a great deal of time and care.
exercise: Ellie's baby cousin grips her finger. Where was he before he was born? | Growing in his mother's womb for about nine months | Waiting in heaven fully grown, then shrinking | Inside a seed that the doctor planted | Nowhere; babies are delivered ready-made by hospitals
exercise: Oscar laughs at a baby photo of himself. Is that really the same Oscar? | Yes, the same person, who has grown and changed ever since | No, babies are secretly swapped for older children at about age five | No, a person becomes someone new every birthday | Only partly, because baby photos are mostly drawings
exercise: Amira's new kittens cannot see. Why? | Kittens are born with their eyes shut, and they open in a week or two | Their eyes are broken and will need an operation | The box under the stairs is too dark for eyes to work | Only the mother cat's eyes work until the kittens are a year old
exercise: Mrs Patel's class plants seeds in yoghurt pots. Why does a dry seed start to grow? | It holds a resting plant and food, and water and warmth wake it | It is dead, and the soil turns it into a new living thing | The yoghurt left in the pot feeds it like milk | It grows only if the whole class prays over it every single morning
exercise: Jack fears his mum will love the new baby more. What is true? | Love grows to cover everyone; a baby needs time, not more love | Each child gets a smaller slice of love as the family grows | Parents always love the youngest child in the family the very most | Older children must earn their parents' love back
exercise: Sophie learned to walk at eleven months. How do babies learn to walk? | By practising and falling over many times | They are born knowing, but wait until they feel like it | Doctors teach them in hospital before they go home | They copy it from cartoons on the television
exercise: Mason sees a baby giraffe walk within an hour. Why are human babies so much slower? | Our big brains have so much to learn as we grow | Human babies are lazier than baby giraffes | Human legs are broken at birth and must mend | Giraffes are taught to walk before they are born
exercise: Leo turns nine. What does a birthday mark? | The day a person was born | The day a person was baptised | The day a person first walked | The day a person started school
exercise: Grace's class studies frogs. What is the right order of the frog's life cycle? | Frogspawn, tadpole, froglet, frog | Tadpole, frogspawn, frog, froglet | Frog, tadpole, frogspawn, froglet | Froglet, frogspawn, tadpole, frog
exercise: Kai's cousin was born early and lies in a special hospital cot. Why? | Early babies often need help to breathe, feed and stay warm | The hospital keeps every baby for a few weeks to check it | She is being punished for arriving too soon | Babies born early can never, ever go home with their own families
exercise: Ruby asks if she was bought as a «gift». Where do babies come from? | A cell from a mum and a cell from a dad join and grow | Parents pick out a baby they like from a shop at the hospital | A stork brings the baby to the right house late at night | Babies grow under the big cabbage leaves in the garden
exercise: A child is adopted into a family. What is true? | The child is just as much part of the family | The child is only a guest until they grow up | The child must go back when the parents have a baby | The child cannot share the family's name
exercise: What do Christians at Ellie's church believe about a new baby? | Every baby is known and loved by God, a gift | Only baptised babies are loved by God | Babies have no worth until they can speak | A baby belongs to the church, not to its family
exercise: How might a non-religious family welcome a new baby? | With a naming day or a party with family and friends | They are not allowed to celebrate a birth at all, by law | By waiting until the child is older and chooses a religion | By holding a baptism in church, but without any water
exercise: What did Jesus say about the mustard seed? | A tiny seed grows into a big plant, so small beginnings matter | Mustard seeds are poisonous and must never be planted | Only big seeds can ever grow into anything that is worth having at all | Seeds are dead, so nothing can come of them
test: Your cousin goes, «babies just appear in the hospital, they build 'em there.» Which reply is true? | A baby grows in its mum's womb for about nine months first | Right, the hospital builds them from parts | Right, but only the girls; boys come by post | Babies grow in eggs that the mum keeps warm
test: A kid in Year 4 says, «that baby's lowkey mid, it can't even walk lol.» («Lowkey» means «a bit»; «mid» means «not great».) What is the fair reply? | Every baby learns to walk slowly by practice; that is normal | Babies who can't walk at birth will never walk | He is right, a good healthy baby should walk within an hour of birth | Babies are born walking but pretend they can't
test: Gran says, «Back in my day we said the stork brought you, pet.» What is the honest truth behind it? | Babies grow inside their mothers; the stork was a story | Storks really did bring babies to families before there were hospitals | Storks still bring babies to families in some faraway countries | The stork story is true, but only for twins and triplets
test: What would happen if a newborn human had to walk away from lions like a baby giraffe? | It could not; human babies need adults to carry and protect them | It would run faster than the giraffe | It would hide in a seed until danger passed | It would learn to walk in five minutes
test: Jack, whose dad is from Newcastle, moans, «Howay man, the bairn gets all the fuss, there'll be nowt left for me.» («Bairn» means «baby»; «nowt» means «nothing».) Which answer is best? | Yes, love is not used up; tell your parents if you feel left out | No, babies always take the love first | Only if you look after the baby every night | Only if the baby turns out to be a girl
test: Amira's little brother, from the Black Country, shouts, «Bostin' kittens! I'm gonna carry 'em round the house!» («Bostin'» means «brilliant».) What is the wise move? | Look gently but let the mother cat feed and care for it | Carry it round so it learns where everything is | Open its eyes for it gently so that it can see much sooner | Give it some milk from the fridge straight away
test: Your Scottish pal says, «Ma wee cousin wis born early, she's in a special cot.» («Wee» means «little».) Is the cousin likely to be all right? | Most early babies grow up strong, though the doctors will say honestly | No, babies born early never survive | Yes, and she can go home the same day | Only if the family moves to a bigger house
test: A cartoon shows a seed lying dead in a drawer until a wizard brings it to life. What does it get wrong? | A seed is alive, resting, and water and warmth wake it | Nothing, seeds really are dead until magic touches them | Seeds only grow if they are kept in drawers | Wizards are needed to plant real seeds
test: Ruby's mate says, «If you're adopted you're not a proper family, no cap.» («No cap» means «no lie».) Which reply is right? | Adopted children are fully part of their families | He is right; only babies born into the family count | Adopted children still belong to the hospital they came from | Families made by adoption only last for a year or so
test: Leo's friend is a Jehovah's Witness and does not come to birthday parties. What is the respectful view? | It is his family's faith and choice, and we respect it | He must be sulking, so ignore him | He is wrong and should be made to come | Birthdays are a law, so he must celebrate
test: «do frogs remember being tadpoles?» asks Grace. What is the most honest answer? | Nobody knows, but it is the same animal that changed shape | Yes, frogs have long talks with each other about it in the pond | No, a frog is a different animal from the tadpole | Frogs forget everything once a day
exam: Ascertain the approximate duration of human gestation prior to birth. | Approximately nine months | Approximately three weeks | Approximately two years | Approximately nine days
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the development of locomotion in the human infant? | It is acquired gradually through repeated practice during the first year | It is fully present at the moment of birth | It is acquired solely through formal instruction by physicians | It is acquired within the first hour, as in the giraffe
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the status of an adopted child. | The adopted child is a full member of the family | The adopted child remains a temporary guest of the household | The adopted child is the property of the adopting parents | The adopted child is excluded from the family's affections
exam: Identify the principal sequence of development in the life cycle of the frog. | Frogspawn, tadpole, froglet, adult frog | Tadpole, adult frog, frogspawn, froglet | Froglet, tadpole, frogspawn, adult frog | Adult frog, froglet, frogspawn, tadpole
